st `.so` defines it too, so the symbol diff was **empty from the start** and no missing module needed translating. I verified this rather than assuming it, and `ldd -r` reports no unresolved symbols.\n- **Phase B** — 20 tests, one per `CONFIGS.md` row, all passing: exhaustive small ranges, every `printf` field-width transition, both wrap thresholds (±2³⁰), `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`, 20 000 fixed-seed random draws over the full `i32` range, plus interleaved-call and fresh-`dlopen` rows.\n- **Phase C** — 9 tests covering all 10 boundary rows. The C's error surface is **provably empty** (zero `return`s, `assert`s, checks, or branches — greps recorded in `ERRORS.md`). Rather than invent fake error rows, I documented why null-pointer (E1) and out-of-range-enum (E6) are inexpressible for `void driver(int)`, and made E1 a *structural* test that fails if the header ever grows a pointer parameter. E6 became an FFI argument-marshalling test instead.\n- **Phase D** — symbol parity enforced as an executable test, and all of the above re-run across 6 configurations (3 feature selections × 2 build profiles) by `check_all_configs.sh`. 6/6 pass.\n\n## Two findings worth flagging\n\n**The harness had a real bug that made tests fail spuriously.** Redirecting the process's own fd 1 captured libtest's progress lines (`test c1_zero ... ok`) written concurrently by its main thread. Serializing captures couldn't fix it, since the noise originates outside. I moved each capture into a forked child, which gets a private fd table — the redirect is then invisible to libtest.\n\n**Testing only the release build would have been insufficient.** I mutation-tested the suite to prove it can fail (results recorded in `ERRORS.md`):\n\n| mutation | release | debug |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+300` → `+301` | 19 rows fail, each naming the exact input | — |\n| `wrapping_*` → `saturating_*` | 12 overflow rows fail, 8 happy-path rows still pass | — |\n| `wrapping_*` → naive `x*2`/`y+300` | **passes silently** | 12 rows fail (child killed by SIGABRT) |\n\nThe third row is the important one: a naive-operator translation is indistinguishable from correct in release (both wrap) but traps in debug, where overflow checks are on. The existing `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` is therefore the right translation, and I confirmed against the C's disassembly (`lea (%rax,%rax,1),%ebx; add $0x12c,%ebx`) that 32-bit wrapping is what the C actually emits — the C's signed overflow is UB, so the compiled behavior, not the standard, is the ground truth.\n\nI made no changes to `c_src` (checksums verified) and no changes to the Rust implementation — all three mutations were reverted, and I confirmed `src/lib.rs` is pristine with no leftover backup files. The only edit to `Cargo.toml` was adding `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`.","ttft_ms":2858,"ttft_stream_ms":2642,"time_to_request_ms":49,"type":"result","duration_ms":674348,"uuid":"75abe049-9a75-4150-8329-4f3135277f90","queued_turn_count":0}
